I. INTRODUCTION
Transient fluctuations of electron content inside regions of the ionosphere interact with propagating radio waves, resulting in scintillation. For global navigation satellite systems (GNSSs) in particular, ionospheric scintillation significantly impact the availability, accuracy, continuity, and integrity of the positioning. The signal processing channels in a GNSS receiver perform carrier and code delay tracking of GNSS satellite signals. Regarding the received signal carrier, ionospheric irregularities are one source of amplitude and phase scintillations adding up to the line-of-sight (LOS) dynamics, introducing disturbances to GNSS tracking algorithms in the receiver that, in many cases, cause a reduction of precision in the positioning and eventually the loss of lock of GNSS signals. Scintillation is more frequently observed at equatorial and high-latitude regions. The most critical effects are induced by severe scintillation activity at equatorial regions, resulting in rapid amplitude and phase scintillation with deep amplitude fades for short periods, depending on the factors, such as solar activity, geomagnetic activity, latitude, carrier frequency, and daytime [1], while at high latitudes, the effects are in general milder and more frequently observed in the phase [2]. The mitigation of the scintillation effects, in addition to their detection and monitoring, is challenging from a signal processing perspective [3]. High accuracy carrier tracking is vital for positioning accuracy improvement in modern receivers applying carrierbased positioning such as real-time kinematics and precise point positioning [4], [5]. Once the scintillation effects are more pronounced in the carrier tracking, robust carrier synchronization structures contemplating the induced phase and amplitude scintillation can significantly improve carrier tracking. Originally, the traditional tracking loop structures implemented as a phase-locked loop (PLL) and/or frequency-locked loop (FLL) are not directly taking into account amplitude or phase scintillation. Their parameters
