 are fixed and adjusted for LOS-only tracking. Robustness can be improved via the tuning of parameters, such as noise bandwidth [6], in PLLs or employing an FLL-assisted PLL structure [7], for example. Other approaches based on the traditional tracking loops use heuristic adjustments [8], [9], extension of the coherent integration time [10], and coupling with inertial sensors or Doppler-aided solutions [11], [12], [13]. The Kalman filter has been successfully employed in GNSS carrier tracking loops to further improve robustness. Early applications involved suboptimal computation of the Kalman gains by solving only the steady-state Riccati equation, as in [14]. Typically, a constant gain Kalman filter structure is chosen in case that simplicity of the suboptimal solution is more suitable for the application than introducing the computational load to perform the real-time update of the gains. Techniques based on the Kalman filtering with update of the gains and considering adaptive update of parameters, typically only the measurement noise and not directly modeling scintillation, were studied in [15], [16], [17], [18], [19], [20], and [21]. A Kalman filter PLL was employed in [22] with LOS and clock error state variables, and measurement noise adjusted as a function of the carrier-to-noise density ratio C/N0, allowing better phase tracking during the power fades and phase dynamics induced by scintillation when compared with a constant bandwidth loop. The introduction of models representing the scintillation dynamics in the received signal into the Kalman filter formulation enabled the decoupling of both scintillation and LOS contributions to overcome the limitations of techniques that only adjust the level of uncertainty in models accounting for states related to the LOS dynamics, e.g., based on an estimate of C/N0 [3]. Scintillation phase and amplitude are typically modeled by autoregressive (AR) processes in Kalman PLLs for scintillation mitigation [23], [24], [25], [26]. In [23], the parameters of the AR model are identified a priori in an offline procedure, the measurement noise covariance is adapted online based on an C/N0 estimator, and a discriminator-based Kalman filter PLL structure estimating the scintillation phase is employed. In [24], an extended Kalman filter PLL structure using the prompt correlator in-phase/quadrature (I/Q) outputs as measurements is used for scintillation mitigation, providing estimates of both scintillation amplitude and phase, with the parameters of their AR models identified offline and adaptive measurement noise covariance update. An extensive evaluation shows that the structure significantly improves the cycle slips and loss of lock robustness while minimizing the carrier phase estimation error, compared with structures not including scintillation modeling. This concept is generalized in [25] with an extended Kalman filter with increased complexity, including an online batch identification of the AR models parameters and online updating of the process noise covariance matrix based on the identification statistics. Other techniques also consider adaptive Kalman filtering with online adaptive estimation
of the AR scintillation model parameters, as in [27], where a dual Kalman filter was applied. In this approach, the scintillation phase and amplitude are estimated by a first Kalman filter. They are then used as measurements in the second Kalman filter to estimate the parameters of the respective AR process. Afterward, the LOS and scintillation phase error estimates are fed back into the carrier signal replica generator by a linear quadratic Gaussian control approach. In this work, we investigate two Kalman PLL structures for ionospheric scintillation mitigation employing radial basis function (RBF) networks instead of the AR models representing scintillation phase and amplitude dynamics, further extending the algorithm presented in [28]. Henceforth, we call the Kalman PLL structures employing the RBF networks as the proposed structures to distinguish them from the analogous structures employing the AR models. In the first structure, the innovations of the Kalman filter are taken from the phase discriminator output and the carrier replica is generated in a numerically controlled oscillator (NCO) with the Kalman filter estimates, such as the discriminator-based Kalman PLL structure of the article presented in [23]. In the second structure, the Kalman filter takes the prompt correlator I/Q outputs as measurements, such as in [25]. In this case, a state feedback controller operating on the error states estimated by the Kalman filter is designed to provide a control signal to the NCO for the carrier replica generation, following a pole placement procedure to compute the gain matrix to drive the closed-loop error dynamics to zero. Both structures have nonlinear process equations due to the RBF networks, and thus, both implement the extended Kalman filter. We propose to employ the RBF networks to model scintillation due to the capability of their nonlinear RBFs to capture eventual nonlinear dynamics, possibly evolving with time, induced by the ionospheric scintillation in the receiver, unlike the AR models. The RBF networks allow greater flexibility in scintillation-induced effects modeling since we can include pure AR terms in their structure in addition to the nonlinear RBFs. Also, we can use recursive least squares to estimate the weights of the networks since they have a linear structure with respect to their weights. The results of the least squares identification are used to update the process equations and process noise covariance matrices of the Kalman filters, as in [25]. The measurement noise covariance matrices are also adaptively computed, as in [25], based on a C/N0 estimator. We assess the performances of the proposed structures employing the RBF networks by computer simulations with synthetic severe scintillation data generated by the Cornell scintillation model (CSM) [29] added to simulated baseband (BB) GPS L1 C/A code signals and with real scintillation data collected by a commercial receiver. We compare the performances to correspondent structures employing AR models.
