V. EVALUATION OF THE PROPOSED KALMAN PLLS WITH RBF NETWORKS MODELING SCINTILLATION
The proposed Kalman PLL structures employing RBF networks modeling scintillation for scintillation mitigation are first evaluated by Monte Carlo simulations considering synthetic scintillation input data generated by the CSM [29] in severe scintillation scenarios also considering receiver thermal noise. The CSM is a statistical model adjusted with empirical data used to generate complex samples of equatorial scintillation time series. It is a popular model, largely employed in the literature [3], and very convenient for simulation purposes [24]. The parameters of the model are the S4 index (0 < S4 ≤ 1), which describes the strength of amplitude scintillation, thus characterizing the amplitude distribution of the time series, and the decorrelation time τ0 (0.1 ≤ τ0 < 2, in seconds), which defines the decay rate of the autocorrelation function of the time series. The rapidity of scintillation increases with the decrease of τ0. The samples are provided at 100 Hz rate. The scintillation is considered weak if S4 ≤ 0.3, moderate if 0.3 < S4 ≤ 0.6, and severe if S4 > 0.6. As a rule, higher S4 and lower τ0 lead to more severe scintillation [23]. Additionally, the Kalman PLLs are evaluated using real scintillation data collected by a commercial off-the-shelf GNSS receiver.
A. Synthetic Scintillation Data
The proposed adaptive Kalman PLL algorithms for scintillation mitigation employing the RBF networks to model scintillation phase and amplitude dynamics are evaluated by numerical simulations considering synthetic scintillation realizations generated by the CSM [29] in a severe scintillation scenario defined by amplitude strength index S4 = 0.8 and decorrelation time τ0 = 0.1 s. We consider receiving an L1 GPS C/A code signal with a Doppler frequency shift of 1 kHz and a Doppler frequency drift of 0.94 Hz/s, which is the maximum value that an on-ground static receiver would experience based on the orbital dynamics of the GPS satellites [47]. Simulated scintillation amplitude and phase with the CSM are introduced to the simulated signal. Perfect code delay and navigation bit synchronization is assumed with the received BB GNSS signals in the receiver and the carrier amplitude is unitary. Gaussian receiver thermal noise is added to the sampled I/Q signal components in all simulations, generated with variance such that the nominal C/N0 is 45 dB-Hz. The samples of the scintillation data are generated by the CSM each 10 ms, and the coherent integration period TI is 10 ms. The performance of the proposed Kalman PLLs employing the RBF networks is evaluated based on 300 Monte
