The performance of the Kalman PLLs is also assessed with real scintillation data collected by a JAVAD Delta 3 receiver [50] connected to a Leica AR25 choke-ring antenna installed on the rooftop of the building of the Department of Teleinformatics Engineering of the Federal University of Ceara (UFC) in Fortaleza, Brazil. The receiver is a part of a scintillation monitoring station operated by the German Aerospace Center (DLR) and UFC. The L1 GPS C/A code signal presenting scintillation from satellite PRN 24, recorded on October 27, 2017, from 22:00 UTC to 23:00 UTC, and from satellite PRN 21, recorded on November 16, 2017, from 22:00 UTC to 23:00 UTC, is used to evaluate the proposed Kalman PLLs. According to the S4 index computed for both datasets, as presented in Fig. 11, PRN 24 oscillates between weak to moderate scintillation, while PRN 21 oscillates between moderate to severe scintillation. The receiver in its default settings implements a third-order PLL with a noise bandwidth of 25 Hz, running at 50 Hz and with TI = 20 ms. Scintillation phase, scintillation amplitude, and carrier time series are obtained from the receiver data using a standard postprocessing procedure, as described in [51] and [52]. In such procedure, the time series are obtained from the postcorrelation internal observables of the receiver tracking loop, such as the I/Q prompt correlator output and the carrier phase estimate, available at 50 Hz. The time series obtained from this procedure are suitable for the evaluation of the algorithms since we work with a postcorrelation signal model considering perfect code delay synchronization. We adjusted the AR and RBF network models to the scintillation phase and amplitude samples obtained from the real scintillation data. The model order, or the number of lags, was chosen to achieve the best fit to the data for both the AR and RBF network models.
