VI. CONCLUSION
In this work, we investigated discriminator-based and correlator-based Kalman PLL structures for ionospheric scintillation mitigation on GNSS receivers, with scintillation phase and amplitude modeled by RBF networks, whose weights can be estimated by recursive sliding window least squares. The performance of the proposed algorithms was assessed by Monte Carlo simulations using synthetic scintillation data and by real scintillation data collected by a commercial receiver. The Monte Carlo simulation results show the capability of the structures employing the RBF networks to perform robust carrier synchronization, with performance similar to the state-of-the-art adaptive approaches using AR scintillation phase and amplitude models, with a slight advantage of the structures employing the RBF networks. The nonlinear RBFs allow to capture eventual nonlinear dynamics induced by scintillation, possibly evolving with time, providing more flexibility than the linear AR models at the expense of a more complex structure. Possibly, the synthetic data considered did not present significant nonlinearities to show this advantage of the RBF networks. On the other hand, the evaluations with real scintillation data highlight the learning and generalization capabilities of the RBF networks to cope with such effects, with the Kalman PLL structures employing the RBF networks presenting reduced error when compared with the structures employing the AR models, when the online parameter estimation was disabled. It was necessary to enable the online parameter estimation so that the AR model could be adjusted to the evolving receiver and scintillation characteristics over time to present errors close to the structures employing the RBF networks.
